<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Daji</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">11th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> · Also known as Su Daji</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Daji was the favourite consort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/di-xin">Di Xin</a>, last king of the Shang dynasty, in traditional Chinese histories. Later writers portrayed her as a cruel woman whose influence led the king into tyranny and brought about the fall of the dynasty, and the Ming novel Investiture of the Gods made her a fox spirit. Historians regard these stories as largely the work of Zhou and later moralists.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/daji.jpg" alt="Daji" width="320" height="588" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photo Dharma from Penang, Malaysia · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ping_Sien_Si_-_026_Daji_(16133466711).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
